<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Red Cloud</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1822–<a href="/years/1909">1909</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Maȟpíya Lúta</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Red Cloud, Maȟpíya Lúta in Lakota, was a leader of the Oglala Lakota from 1865 to 1909. He led the Lakota to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Army in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-clouds-war">Red Cloud's War</a>, whose largest action, the Fetterman Fight of 1866, killed 81 American soldiers. After signing the Treaty of Fort Laramie in 1868 he led his people through the transition to reservation life. Some of his opponents wrongly took him for the overall leader of the Sioux, whose divisions were highly decentralised, with bands acting on their own under leaders respected as warriors, as he was.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/red-cloud.jpg" alt="Red Cloud" width="330" height="540" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Karl Hillers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_Cloud_by_John_K_Hillers_circa_1880.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…an orthography in her fieldwork of the 1930s that anticipated much of the Standard Lakota Orthography, now the basis of the New Lakota Dictionary, with some 43,000 entries, and taught at Sitting Bull College, the Oglala Lakota County School District and Maȟpíya Lúta Owáyawa, formerly <b>Red Cloud</b> School.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
